 "etapas": [
  {
   "etapa": "explicar",
   "piezas": [
    {
     "tipo": "explicacion",
     "titulo": "You read it as it is written",
     "texto": [
      "In Spanish almost every letter has **just one sound**. Today you learn the most useful ones:\n\n- **h** is never pronounced: **hola** sounds like **ola**.\n- **ñ** is a letter of its own: **año**, **niño**.\n- **b** and **v** sound the same: **vaso**, **barco**.\n- One **r** between vowels is soft (**pero**); **rr** rolls strongly (**perro**).\n\nIf you read each syllable slowly, you can already say almost any word."
     ],
     "tutora": ""
    },
    {
     "tipo": "gramatica",
     "titulo": "One letter, one sound (almost always)",
     "texto": [
      "Some letters change depending on the vowel after them:\n\n- **c** + a, o, u sounds like **k**: **casa**. **c** + e, i sounds like **th** in Spain: **cine**.\n- **qu** + e, i sounds like **k** and the u is silent: **queso**.\n- **g** + e, i sounds like **j**: **gente**. For the soft sound you write **gu**: **guitarra**.\n\nWith these three rules you can read almost every word."
     ],
